---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import ForFramework from '@/components/ForFramework.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';

const readInspect = `import { inspect } from '@udixio/motion/scope';

const report = inspect();
// The policy says whether the page reduces motion, and where that setting comes from.
console.log(report.policy);
// Each binding, with its element, shows what plays and what is held.
console.table(report.bindings);`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="inspect"
  title="Motion: inspect and revert"
  description="The functions of @udixio/motion/scope: inspect() reports the motions of a page or a section in development, and revert() tears a section's motions down. In HTML, React, Angular and Svelte."
  frameworkSwitch
>
  <ForFramework as="span" slot="summary">
    <Fragment slot="html">Pass a section to <code>inspect()</code> to read what plays there, and to <code>revert()</code> to tear its motions down.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="react">Pass a section's <code>ref</code> element to <code>inspect()</code> to read what plays there, and to <code>revert()</code> to tear its motions down.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="angular">Pass a section's element, read with <code>viewChild</code>, to <code>inspect()</code> to read what plays there, and to <code>revert()</code> to tear its motions down.</Fragment>
    <Fragment slot="svelte">Pass a section's element, bound with <code>bind:this</code>, to <code>inspect()</code> to read what plays there, and to <code>revert()</code> to tear its motions down.</Fragment>
  </ForFramework>

  <p>Both functions come from the import path <code>@udixio/motion/scope</code>, which a page loads only when you import it.</p>

  <h2>Tear a section down with revert()</h2>
  <ForFramework>
    <p slot="html">Pass the section's element to <code>revert()</code>, such as <code>document.getElementById('gallery')</code>. The example's Remove the motions button does that.</p>
    <p slot="react">Put a <code>ref</code> on the section, and pass its <code>current</code> element to <code>revert()</code>. The example's Remove the motions button does that.</p>
    <p slot="angular">Read the section with <code>viewChild</code>, and pass its <code>nativeElement</code> to <code>revert()</code>. The example's Remove the motions button does that.</p>
    <p slot="svelte">Bind the section with <code>bind:this</code>, and pass the element to <code>revert()</code>. The example's Remove the motions button does that.</p>
  </ForFramework>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-scopes-breakpoints" live={false} />
  <p>
    <code>revert(root)</code> destroys every motion under <code>root</code>, the root included: presence, emphasis, layout, reveal, scrub,
    loop, values, gestures and sequences. Each element is left in its stylesheet state, and the section keeps its markup. Use it to tear
    down a section of a page, or in a test.
  </p>

  <h2>Read a section with inspect()</h2>
  <p>
    <code>inspect(root)</code> returns a plain report for development: the reduced-motion policy and where it comes from, each binding under
    <code>root</code> with its element, kind, name, verb and state, and the defined motions. It changes nothing on the page. Pass an
    element to read only its section.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={readInspect} data-language="ts" />
  <p>In a development build, importing <code>@udixio/motion/scope</code> also sets the report as <code>udixio.motion.inspect</code>.</p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>If a reverted section must move again, make a connection after the revert: a connection made afterwards binds the section again.</li>
  </ul>
</MotionPage>
